{"count": 41706, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/works/?page=2", "previous": null, "results": [{"id": 125419, "acmi_id": "199126", "title": "Collector's Cabinet: Australian Tennis Games", "title_annotation": "", "slug": "display-group-collectors-cabinet-australian-tennis", "creator_credit": "", "credit_line": "", "headline_credit": "", "video_links": [], "record_type": "group", "type": "Object", "is_on_display": false, "last_on_display_place": null, "last_on_display_date": null, "is_context_indigenous": false, "material_description": "", "unpublished": false, "external": false, "first_production_date": null, "commissioned": false, "public_domain": false, "external_references": [], "brief_description": "<p>Tennis is one of the oldest videogame genres – one of the world’s first games was Tennis for Two (1958). From the earliest days of play, the sport has tested the limits of technology and design, whether through Pong (1972) or Wii Sports (2006).</p>", "constellations_primary": [{"id": 424, "name": "Racquets and rivalries", "description": "From drawing on player likenesses to fictionalising the drama of historic matches, explore how tennis has inspired screen makers to bring the game to life on screen."}], "constellations_other": [], "recommendations": [], "title_for_label": "<p>Collector&#8217;s Cabinet: Australian Tennis Games</p>", "creator_credit_for_label": "", "headline_credit_for_label": "", "description": "<p>Tennis is one of the oldest videogame genres – one of the world’s first games was Tennis for Two (1958). From the earliest days of play, the sport has tested the limits of technology and design, whether through Pong (1972) or Wii Sports (2006).</p>\n<p>From the build-it-yourself Selecta-game sports console to homegrown champions starring on international videogame covers, Australian tennis players and developers have left their mark on the genre.</p>\n<p>In 2018, Melbourne’s Big Ant Studios released AO International Tennis, the first licensed Australian Open videogame and the first to use real-match statistical data to mirror the play styles of twenty stars, including Rafael Nadal, Angelique Kerber, Naomi Osaka and Ash Barty, who features on the sequel’s cover. Australia’s contribution to the genre reflects the deep cultural significance of tennis national life – it’s no wonder the Australian Open is known as “the happy slam”.</p>", "description_for_label": ["<p>Tennis is one of the oldest videogame genres – one of the world’s first games was Tennis for Two (1958). From the earliest days of play, the sport has tested the limits of technology and design, whether through Pong (1972) or Wii Sports (2006).</p>\n<p>From the build-it-yourself Selecta-game sports console to homegrown champions starring on international videogame covers, Australian tennis players and developers have left their mark on the genre.</p>\n<p>In 2018, Melbourne’s Big Ant Studios released AO International Tennis, the first licensed Australian Open videogame and the first to use real-match statistical data to mirror the play styles of twenty stars, including Rafael Nadal, Angelique Kerber, Naomi Osaka and Ash Barty, who features on the sequel’s cover. Australia’s contribution to the genre reflects the deep cultural significance of tennis national life – it’s no wonder the Australian Open is known as “the happy slam”.</p>"], "credit_line_for_label": "", "details": [{"label": "Object Types", "display_values": ["Group"]}], "stats": {"tap_count": 3}, "links": [], "creators_primary": [], "creators_other": [{"id": 3109038, "name": "Big Ant Studios", "creator_id": 89086, "creator_wikidata_id": null, "role": "", "role_id": 604, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 3109040, "name": "Orange Pictures", "creator_id": 89088, "creator_wikidata_id": null, "role": "", "role_id": 604, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 3109039, "name": "Zuma Press", "creator_id": 89087, "creator_wikidata_id": null, "role": "", "role_id": 604, "is_primary": false}], "media_note": "Sorry, we don't have images or video for this item.", "holdings": [], "part_of": null, "parts": [], "part_siblings": [], "group": null, "group_works": [], "group_siblings": [], "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "199126", "eaas_environment_id": null, "production_places": [], "production_dates": [], "labels": [64879]}, {"id": 77913, "acmi_id": "016309", "title": "The Devil's Playground", "title_annotation": "", "slug": "the-devils-playground", "creator_credit": "Fred Schepisi and Feature Film House Productions", "credit_line": "", "headline_credit": "Australia, 1976", "video_links": [], "record_type": "work", "type": "Film", "is_on_display": false, "last_on_display_place": null, "last_on_display_date": null, "is_context_indigenous": false, "material_description": "", "unpublished": false, "external": false, "first_production_date": "1976-01-01T11:00:00+11:00", "commissioned": false, "public_domain": false, "external_references": [{"id": 53317, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q3520601"}], "brief_description": "<p><i>The Devil&#8217;s Playground</i> is a 1976 Australian drama film written, produced and directed by Fred Schepisi. It is a semi-autobiographical film which tells the story of a boy growing up and going to school in a Catholic juniorate administered by De La Salle Brothers.</p>", "constellations_primary": [], "constellations_other": [{"id": 272, "name": "Fred Schepisi", "description": "Fred Schepisi's diverse body of work connects to acclaimed local productions, westerns and Hollywood favourites."}], "recommendations": [], "title_for_label": "<p>The Devil&#8217;s Playground</p>", "creator_credit_for_label": "<p>Fred Schepisi and Feature Film House Productions</p>", "headline_credit_for_label": "<p>Australia, 1976</p>", "description": "<p>The subject of a Catholic adolescence in a preparatory school for seminarians set in provincial Victoria, 1953, is substantially autobiographical which explains the engaging intimacy that Schepisi achieves in his first film. An unusual feature, given its subject, is the avoidance of a single point of view. It is not just the youths that are trapped by the Irish Catholic rigidities of the institution but the staff as well. The film does not question belief so much as the institutional distortions that are engendered by the imperatives of belief. Cast includes Arthur Dignam, Nick Tate, Simon Burke and Thomas Keneally.</p>", "description_for_label": ["<p>The subject of a Catholic adolescence in a preparatory school for seminarians set in provincial Victoria, 1953, is substantially autobiographical which explains the engaging intimacy that Schepisi achieves in his first film. An unusual feature, given its subject, is the avoidance of a single point of view. It is not just the youths that are trapped by the Irish Catholic rigidities of the institution but the staff as well. The film does not question belief so much as the institutional distortions that are engendered by the imperatives of belief. Cast includes Arthur Dignam, Nick Tate, Simon Burke and Thomas Keneally.</p>"], "credit_line_for_label": "", "details": [{"label": "Duration", "display_values": ["01:38:00:00"]}, {"label": "Language", "display_values": ["English"]}, {"label": "Audience classification", "display_values": ["MA"]}, {"label": "Subject categories", "display_values": ["Economics, Philosophy, Politics, Religion & Sociology&nbsp;→ Adolescence", "Economics, Philosophy, Politics, Religion & Sociology&nbsp;→ Catholic Church", "Family, Gender Identity, Relationships & Sexuality&nbsp;→ Adolescence", "Feature films", "Feature films&nbsp;→ Feature films - Australia"]}, {"label": "Sound/audio", "display_values": ["Sound"]}, {"label": "Colour", "display_values": ["Colour"]}], "stats": {"tap_count": 134}, "links": [{"url": "https://www.justwatch.com/au/movie/the-devils-playground", "title": "Stream, rent or buy at <span>JustWatch</span>", "source": "justwatch"}], "creato